Even though there's not much memory or speed, it's still nice having reliable bullet-proof software.  At the time, many (most) programmers were skilled amateurs who never touched a mainframe or a VAX, and the users of a lot of the software were not programmers at all.  "turn key" systems were common - put in a disk, and now you're in the environment of the application.  (consistent UI was still a thing that wouldn't occur for a bunch of years)\
\
You can write software, even in BASIC, that does error checking of input, error checking of disk I/O, as well has tricks for improving performance (often at the expense of software readability) by placing variables and subroutines at specific places in your program.\
